WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
The shared responsibility model is a practical starting point for cloud incident response.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:12.891
It clarifies which responsibilities generally belong to the cloud provider and which belong to the customer.

3
00:00:12.891 --> 00:00:19.072
The provider is typically responsible for the security of the underlying cloud infrastructure.

4
00:00:19.072 --> 00:00:28.646
The customer is typically responsible for how accounts, identities, workloads, networks, storage, data, and applications are configured and used.

5
00:00:28.646 --> 00:00:31.436
The exact boundary changes by service type.

6
00:00:31.436 --> 00:00:41.160
Infrastructure as a service gives customers more direct responsibility for operating systems, network rules, workload configuration, and patching.

7
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:51.940
Platform services shift some operational responsibility to the provider but still require secure configuration, identity control, data protection, and monitoring.

8
00:00:51.940 --> 00:01:01.288
Serverless, containers, managed databases, identity providers, and SaaS platforms each change what responders can directly inspect or change.

9
00:01:01.288 --> 00:01:08.450
Scope is also defined by account, subscription, project, tenant, region, environment, and service ownership.

10
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:13.576
A security team may have access to central logs but not to every workload account.

11
00:01:13.576 --> 00:01:17.195
A development team may own an application subscription.

12
00:01:17.195 --> 00:01:20.285
A SaaS administrator may control audit exports.

13
00:01:20.285 --> 00:01:29.332
During an incident, responders need to know where authority begins, where escalation is required, and which teams own the affected services.

14
00:01:29.332 --> 00:01:32.799
Misunderstanding responsibility can slow response.

15
00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:39.885
If the customer assumes the provider will detect every exposed storage object, important alerts may be missed.

16
00:01:39.885 --> 00:01:49.535
If the responder assumes they can acquire evidence the provider controls, the team may waste time instead of opening the right support or legal channel.

17
00:01:49.535 --> 00:01:53.983
Clear responsibility mapping helps set realistic response actions.

18
00:01:53.983 --> 00:02:07.929
A strong cloud response plan identifies what the team can control directly, what must be coordinated with service owners, what requires provider support, and what requires legal, privacy, or compliance coordination.

19
00:02:07.929 --> 00:02:14.110
This prevents responders from improvising authority boundaries during a high-pressure event.
